.TH G3PROBE 1
.SH NAME
g3probe \- compare the 3-D providers on one face at a time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B g3probe
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I G3probe
draws the same small scenes through
.B /dis/math/draw3d.dis
and
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
and counts what lands, so that a difference between the two is a number
rather than an impression.
It exists because
.IR draw3dbench (1)
checks the GPU's flat
.B fillpoly3
for placement, scale, rotation, occlusion and equal depth, and checks
.B fillpoly3g
\- the per-vertex entry point that
.IR gorge (1)
and
.IR strider (1)
are built almost entirely on \- for nothing but whether it shades.
.PP
Four measurements, each for both providers:
.TP
depth
one quad at six depths, through
.B fillpoly3
and
.BR fillpoly3g ,
lit pixels each.
The two entry points must agree.
.TP
occlusion
a near green quad and a far red one, drawn in both orders, through
.B frustum
and through
.BR frustumoffset .
With a working depth buffer the order cannot matter.
.TP
scale
a corridor of 1 to 256 faces, the shape
.I gorge
draws.
.TP
frames
the same corridor drawn over one frame and over eight, with a present
between each.
.TP
model
the corridor again with a non-identity model matrix, since everything
else leaves it at identity and the games do not.
.TP
eye plane
a wall running from behind the eye to well in front, as
.IR gorge (1)'s
canyon does every frame, with
.B fillpoly3g
and with
.BR line3 ;
and then one
.B fillpoly3
quad that genuinely straddles the plane.
The wall is made of quads stepping by 0.4 from 0.8, so their edges land
.I on
the eye plane rather than across it \- every one of them is wholly in
front or wholly behind, and a test made of them cannot tell a straddling
polygon that is clipped from one that is dropped.
.TP
window clip
a small window in the middle of the screen with everything behind it
painted black, and geometry aimed past its edge, through each entry
point in turn:
.BR fillpoly3g ,
.BR fillpoly3 ,
.BR line3 ,
.B plot3
and
.BR sprite3 .
Two counts, the lit pixels inside the window and the lit pixels
anywhere else on the screen.
The second must be zero: a draw touches nothing outside its
destination's clip rectangle, and a program that paints over its
neighbours is not sharing a screen.
.PP
It also writes the first device frame to
.B g3probe.raw
as raw pixels in the display's format, for looking at.
.SH RESULTS
Placement, the depth test,
.BR frustumoffset ,
a model transform and two hundred and fifty-six faces all agree between
providers to within about 1%, which is rasterisation.
.PP
The two that did not are why this exists.
A wall crossing the eye plane lit 40596 pixels through software and
.B 185898
through Metal until
.I devdraw
was made to clip Gouraud polygons the way it always clipped flat ones;
it is 40290 now.
.PP
The other was the Limbo provider, which had no near-plane clipping at
all.
.I Project
returns not-ok for a vertex behind the eye and every caller returned on
the first one, so a polygon or a line with any vertex behind the viewer
was dropped whole \- and a wall running from behind the viewer to in
front of them is what a corridor is made of.
A single straddling quad lit 0 pixels through software and 56120 through
the device; a line through the eye plane lit 0 and 25481.
.I Devdraw 's
C path has clipped both since it was written.
With the same cut in
.B /appl/math/draw3d.b
they are 56612 against 56120 and 25570 against 25481, which is
rasterisation.
.PP
A single frame drawn and read back used to give nothing at all about
half the time \- presentation consumed the geometry queue without
writing the softscreen back, so whether a frame's 3-D reached the image
everything else reads was a race between the present and the next
readback.
Eight frames won it eventually; one frame often did not.
That is fixed:
.I metal_flush_geom
reads the geometry back as part of snapshotting, so materialising is
no longer a race, and eight runs of the one-frame case now agree
exactly.
.PP
The window clip is the most recent, and it did not agree.
With the device provider, geometry aimed past the edge of a 300 by 240
window put pixels on the screen outside it \- 2500 from
.BR fillpoly3 ,
2500 from
.BR fillpoly3g ,
3120 from
.BR sprite3 ,
435 from
.B line3
and 243 from
.B plot3
\- where the software provider put none.
The Metal passes never consulted the destination's clip rectangle:
.I poly_pixdst
translated a window's coordinates onto the physical screen and then had
nothing left to say where the window had been.
Every queued primitive now carries that rectangle and each pass sets it
as the scissor, and all five read zero.
.SH CONTROLS
.B INFERNO_NOGPU
takes the GPU away entirely, so the device provider falls back on
.IR devdraw 's
own C rasteriser.
.PP
.B INFERNO_METAL_NOTL
takes away the transform-and-light entry points and leaves the rest of
the GPU hooked.
For
.B fillpoly3
each vertex is then projected in C and the face still reaches the GPU,
by the screen-space triangle queue instead of the transform one \- so
both routes end on the same Metal pipeline, and a picture that changes
between them is the GPU's own vertex stage.
.PP
.B Fillpoly3g
is the exception, and it matters when reading the table above:
.IR devdraw 's
Gouraud case is a parallel block with its own projection and fill, and
does not fall through to the screen-space queue.
Under
.B INFERNO_METAL_NOTL
it is drawn on the CPU, so a
.B fillpoly3g
row taken under that variable says nothing about the GPU.
.B Line3 ,
.B plot3
and
.B sprite3
are not affected by it at all.
.PP
The counters say which happened.
.B INFERNO_METAL_STATS
prints
.B g3_calls
for the transform-and-light queue and
.B fill_calls
for the screen-space one: one run of this program gives 2752 and 0 by
default, and 0 and 6 with
.B INFERNO_METAL_NOTL
set.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/g3probe.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3dbench (1),
.IR framegrab (1),
.IR gorge (1)
.PP
.B /doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
.SH BUGS
It needs a window and no window manager: with no
.I wm
the display is what the program drew into, which is what makes reading
it back mean anything.
.PP
It flushes before every read.
Without that it measures the screen as it stood before the drawing, and
an early version of this reported a first-call fault that was entirely
its own missing flush.
.PP
The window clip test makes its own window rather than reshaping the
one it started with.
With no window manager under it,
.IR wmclient (2)
gives a window the size of the display whatever size is asked for \- and
geometry cannot escape a window that is already the whole screen.
An earlier version reshaped, got a full-screen window, and reported no
escape from a renderer that was escaping.
.PP
Nothing here asserts.
It prints numbers for a person to compare, because two of the four
measurements have no single right answer to check against \- only the
other provider.
